    61	| **C1D** | C1 + DC-filter boundary only (attention breaks remain) | loose |
    62	| **D** | A + fused RMSNorm (`frontend_rms_fused.rms_fused`, the owner's Triton kernel) in `band_split` via the `FusedBandSplit` swap from `arms/common.py`; eager elsewhere | tight |
    63	| **E** | D + whole-forward capture (B's ladder) | tight |
    64	| **F** | A + multi-stream band loop in `band_split` (`arms/streams.py`, 4 streams, fork/join events) | tight |
    65	| **G** | D + F + capture: the stream-ordered structure captured into ONE graph (B's ladder) | tight |
    66	| **H** | C3 + **pad-and-batch band modules** (§1.1). Compiled with the C3 call. Generic reimplementation of the band-bmm idea; production code is **not used** (not available to the agent; the owner may substitute it and say so) | loose |
    67	
    68	`C1A`/`C1D` run at 352800 only (they isolate the graph-break tax at the
    69	deployed operating point; plan 1.2). All other arms run at both operating
    70	points. The chunk sweep (22050, 88200) runs A, C3, G, H only (plan 1.1).
    71	
    72	### 1.1 Arm H — pad-and-batch, exactness argument
    73	- `band_split`: the 60 ragged bands (K_i ∈ dim_inputs) are gathered into one
    74	  zero-padded tensor `(T, 60, K_max)` by a single precomputed index_select
    75	  through an appended zero column; `F.normalize(dim=-1)` over the padded row
    76	  equals the per-band normalize (zeros add nothing to the L2 norm); the
    77	  per-band scale `K_i**0.5` is a `(60,1,1)` vector; gamma is zero-padded;
    78	  `bmm` with zero-padded weights `(60, K_max, 384)` + bias; output permuted to
    79	  `(1, T, 60, 384)` = the stock `torch.stack` order. Padded rows contribute
    80	  exactly 0; the only numerical difference is cuBLAS summation order
    81	  (loose tier by the rule).
    82	- `mask_estimators[·]`: the 60 per-band `MLP(384→1536→1536→2K_i)+GLU` chains
    83	  become three batched `bmm`s (`(60,T,384)@(60,384,1536)`, tanh,
    84	  `@(60,1536,1536)`, tanh, `@(60,1536,2K_max)`), where the last weight's
    85	  columns are laid out as `[a-half padded to K_max | b-half padded to K_max]`
    86	  so `GLU(dim=-1)` on the padded width is exact (pad columns: `0·σ(0)=0`);
    87	  valid columns are gathered back to the flat `(1, T, ΣK_i)` by one
    88	  precomputed index_select. Padded weights are derived buffers built once from
    89	  the original parameters (no parameter is modified).
    90	- If any of these exactness claims fails validation, H is INVALID and the
    91	  failure is reported — it is not tuned.
    92	
    93	### 1.2 Planted-fault validator check (registered prediction; runs first)
    94	Arm A with `--planted-fault`: the wrapper adds the constant `1e-3` to the
    95	output audio. **Prediction: validation FAILS on both seeds with a
    96	seed-invariant `max_abs_diff` = 1e-3 (to fp32 rounding).** A validator that
    97	passes this run invalidates every validation verdict of the session.
    98	
    99	## 2. Task-quality gate (plan 0.2)
   100	- 8 fixed clips, 8.000 s each (= one 352800-sample chunk), from the
   101	  MUSDB18-HQ **test** split via the public HF mirror
   102	  `roro128/musdb18-hq-flac` at revision `32fd4e850b45ea0b635be067b921661984b53029`
   103	  (lossless FLAC re-encode of `danjacobellis/musdb18HQ`; CC BY-NC-SA 4.0;
   104	  research use only; **audio is never committed** — `configs/sdr_clips.json`
   105	  records track, split, clip start sample, and the sha256 of every derived
   106	  wav; `scripts/prepare_sdr_clips.py` regenerates the pack deterministically:
   107	  the first 8 tracks of shards `test-00000/00001` in shard order, clip start =
   108	  the largest multiple of 441 ≤ 30 % of the track length).
   109	- Per clip and arm, at the 352800 operating point: separated vocals
   110	  `est`; `SDR(ref, est) = 10·log10(‖ref‖² / ‖ref − est‖²)` (plain, no
   111	  BSS-eval projections) and `SI-SDR(ref, est)`, computed (a) vs the reference
   112	  vocals stem and (b) vs the eager arm A's output for the same clip
   113	  (`SDR(A_out, est)` — the output-fidelity number).
   114	- **Gate (common to every arm compared on speed):** mean over the 8 clips of
   115	  `ΔSDR_ref = SDR_ref(arm) − SDR_ref(A) ≥ −0.1 dB`. The per-clip minimum,
   116	  SI-SDR, and the vs-A numbers are reported alongside; the gate is the mean.
   117	  Tolerance tiers remain the numerical-equivalence check; SDR is the
   118	  application gate. At chunk sizes other than 352800 only the tier check
   119	  applies (every arm transformation is chunk-independent; a chunk-specific
   120	  failure would show in the tier check at that chunk).
   121	- The gate is computed in the same process as restart `r1` of each arm at
   122	  352800 (after the timed region), so no extra model builds are needed.
   123	
   124	## 3. Graph-deployment verification (plan 0.3)
   125	"Zero graph breaks" is not accepted as evidence of full capture. For C1, C3,
   126	H (Inductor cudagraph trees) and B, E, G (manual graphs), record per pass,
   127	from the node-level nsys export of session 2 (`cuda_api_trace` +
   128	`cuda_gpu_trace`, capture window = the 5a latency block):
   129	`cudaGraphLaunch` calls/pass, `cudaLaunchKernel`-family calls/pass outside
   130	graph replay (eager launches), memcpy/memset API calls/pass, distinct kernel
   131	names inside vs outside replay; plus, for the Inductor arms, the
   132	`torch._inductor` cudagraph counters (`cudagraph_skips` and reasons,
   133	`TORCH_LOGS=cudagraphs`) and the cudagraph-trees recorded-graph count.
   134	An arm is reported **fully graphed** only if eager kernel launches/pass = 0;
   135	otherwise the uncaptured ops are listed by name. No expectation is
   136	registered; the numbers are the deliverable.
   137	
   138	## 4. Boundary-cost microbenchmark (plan 0.4; design registered, owner reviews)
   139	`fullmodel/boundary_bench.py`: chains of N **dependent** trivial Triton
   140	kernels (each loads one element, adds 1, stores it back — same buffer, so
   141	kernel k+1 depends on kernel k), N ∈ {10, 100, 1000, 2000}, under
   142	(a) stream launches, (b) one CUDA graph of the chain (replayed),
   143	(c) programmatic dependent launch (Triton 3.4 `launch_pdl=True` with
   144	`gdc_wait()`/`gdc_launch_dependents()`) on a stream and inside a graph;
   145	plus chains of small *real* kernels — an in-place elementwise op on a
   146	37 MB fp16 tensor, and alternating dependent GEMMs 660×384×1536 /
   147	660×1536×384 fp16. Device time via CUDA events around the chain, median of
   148	20 repeats, min/max spread; `c_boundary(N, mode) = chain time / N`, with the
   149	single-kernel duration from the node-level trace reported separately so the
   150	per-boundary overhead can be separated from the kernel's own execution.
   151	Prior calibration points to compare against: Hazy (H100 ≈ 2.1 µs stream,
   152	≈ 1.3 µs graph); NVIDIA CUDA 12.6 straight-line graph ≈ 0.55 µs/node (RTX
   153	3060). **Until measured, no per-boundary number is used anywhere** (the
   154	ledger's X10 uses only the measured value). Any mode that is unavailable on
   155	the pod is recorded `UNTESTED(reason)`.
   156	
   157	## 5. Session structure, protocol, budget
   158	- **Protocol 5a**, reused from `arms/common.py::timed_run` (per-iteration
   159	  synchronize latency block of 50 passes inside the cudaProfilerApi range;
   160	  one free-running block of 50 passes outside it), **five repeats per
   161	  process**; per-process statistic = min-of-medians and median spread; per
   162	  (arm × chunk) headline = **median over 3 fresh-process restarts of the
   163	  per-process min-of-medians**, every per-iteration list preserved
   164	  (`sm120` deviation 5 carried over). Restart rounds are **interleaved**
   165	  (round-major: r1 of every arm, then r2, then r3) so C1/C3/C1D/C3-class
   166	  comparisons are matched in time and clocks (plan 1.2). Clocks logged in
   167	  the background at 1 Hz (`nvidia-smi --query-gpu=clocks.sm,clocks.mem,
   168	  power.draw --format=csv -l 1`) for the whole session; timed blocks carry
   169	  wall timestamps.
   170	- **Unprofiled timing is the primary result.** Session-2 traces are for
   171	  mechanism; profiled and unprofiled numbers never share a ratio. Profiling
   172	  perturbation (plan 1.6): A, C3, G at 4410 and 352800, wall unprofiled vs
   173	  `--cuda-graph-trace=graph` vs `=node`; >5 % inflation → every mechanism
   174	  number from that trace carries the factor as a caveat.
   175	- **Session 1 (≤ 3 h, RunPod secure-cloud RTX 5090, pod name
   176	  `melband_profiling`, image `runpod/pytorch:1.1.0-rc.154-cu1281-torch280-ubuntu2204`
   177	  = the pinned torch 2.8.0+cu128 / triton 3.4.0 matrix):** setup (idempotent
   178	  `setup.sh` + full-model deps + clip pack + nsys), planted fault, the arm
   179	  matrix at 352800 and 4410 (3 interleaved restarts; C2 last with the 25-min
   180	  cap), SDR gate in r1, evidence extraction before terminate.
   181	- **Session 2 (≤ 3 h):** chunk sweep (A, C3, G, H × {22050, 88200}, 3
   182	  restarts), node-level traces of A, A-NVTX, C3, G, H at both operating
   183	  points, profiling-perturbation runs, attention-backend microbench (plan
   184	  1.5: time-axis batch 60 × seq 801 and freq-axis batch 801 × seq 60,
   185	  heads 8, dim_head 64, fp16, flash / mem-efficient / cuDNN / math, at both
   186	  operating points' frame counts), boundary microbench (§4), exports,
   187	  evidence extraction.
   188	- Engineering shakedown of the harness on ml-beast GPU 4 is permitted before
   189	  session 1 (the `sm120` smoke rule): its outputs are never session evidence
   190	  and are never cited.
   191	- Budget guard: every process runs under `timeout`; a timed-out or crashed
   192	  run is INVALID/UNTESTED in its JSON and the session continues (loud in the
   193	  console log, never silent).
   194	
   195	## 6. Exploratory hypothesis (labelled exploratory; directional only)
   196	At the 4410 operating point, fusion and concurrency (arms G, H) give a wall
   197	speedup **beyond** graphed compilation (best of B, C1, C3). No numeric ratio
