Methodology constraints (violating any invalidates the run)
All arms, same instance, same session. Separate OS process per chunk regime.
 torch.compile(..., dynamic=False) ; let autotune fully settle before timing.
 TORCH_LOGS=perf_hints (and recompile logs) captured on every compile arm.
Graphs mechanics: static I/O buffers, .copy_() into them, warmup on side stream, capture OUTSIDE the
timed region.
≥ 50 timed iters after warmup; per-iteration times printed; median + min/max; ≥ 3 process restarts for the
headline numbers. 5a. TIMING PROTOCOL (clarification registered 2026-08-16, pre-data): "wall" for the
decision rule = per-iteration latency with torch.cuda.synchronize() after each pass. Rationale: streaming
semantics — chunks arrive on real-time cadence, back-to-back pipelining is unavailable in deployment, so
latency (not throughput) is the decision quantity in the 4410 regime. Each arm×regime ALSO records one
free-running block (sync, N iters, sync; total/N) as throughput_ms_per_pass — the honest metric for the
offline 8 s regime, the continuity bridge to the historical free-running baseline (5.5–7.5 ms), and the empirical
measure of the serialization delta. Known bias: per-iteration sync can only inflate G's wall → inflates R →
biases AGAINST the kill verdict (toward candidate (a) surviving); expected < 2%. Escalation clause: if the
free-running delta at the 4410 regime exceeds 5%, the decision rule is evaluated under BOTH protocols and
any disagreement between their verdicts is escalated, not silently resolved.
≥ 2-pass different-data validation on every arm — including D/E/G (stale-buffer aliasing under capture
passes single-pass checks while being wrong).
First action of the session, before any timing: torch.compile output_code reads at both chunk sizes —
kernel inventory recorded.
nsys timeline of (best generic arm) and (G): kernel count + gap structure. These two traces are the structural
evidence either way.
P8 — full-model fraction (registered 2026-08-15, unmeasured on clean provenance). Session opener of
null-test day, before any arms: one nsys trace of the FULL forward, 8 s chunk. Prediction: front-end fraction
on the 5090 comes out ABOVE the ~5% work-data figure from the A5000 production host — ~8–15%.
Mechanism: the numerator (front-end) is host-dispatch-bound and does not scale with the card; the
denominator (bandwidth-bound transformer stack) shrinks with 1792 vs 768 GB/s. Also recorded from this
trace: kernel-count and gap structure of the attention stack (candidate (b)'s first evidence). Gate-memo
obligation attached: pin the quality-viable streaming operating point (likely 0.5–2 s hops, not 100 ms); the
launch-cost argument is monotonic in chunk size and survives wherever the pin lands.
